 title:'America Acquired a Brazilian Rare-Earth Mine. Who Gets the Supply?',
 deck:'USA Rare Earth closed its Serra Verde acquisition, but the path from a producing Brazilian mine to American-made magnets remains unproven.',
 image: { src: 'assets/images/epnews_brazil_serra_verde_approved_1600x900.jpg', alt: 'EP News informational graphic about the Serra Verde rare-earth mine in Goiás, Brazil', credit: 'EP News AI-generated editorial illustration; not a documentary photograph of the Serra Verde mine.' },
 body:["The deal is closed: USA Rare Earth said it completed its combination with Serra Verde on September 3, 2026. The Goiás operation began commercial production in 2024, before the latest U.S. hemispheric strategy. The April agreement valued the transaction at approximately $2.8 billion using the company's then-current stock price; that was not a $2.8 billion cash payment.",
"What is being produced?: Serra Verde produces rare-earth material containing magnet-relevant elements, including dysprosium and terbium. The company targeted an approximately 4,000-ton annual total rare-earth-oxide run rate by late 2026 and a later 6,400-ton expansion. These are targets, not independently measured annual output.",
"Follow the financing: The company disclosed a $565 million U.S. International Development Finance Corporation financing package for optimization and expansion. A separate $1.55 billion of capitalization arrangements for an offtake vehicle included a $750 million U.S. defense investment, a bank commitment letter for up to $500 million subject to conditions, and at least $300 million of government forward purchases over five years. These are different financial instruments, not interchangeable cash expenditures.",
"Who gets the supply?: The disclosed 15-year offtake arrangement covers all of Serra Verde's Phase 1 magnetic-rare-earth production. That can give U.S.-linked buyers contractual access, but it does not establish how much material has reached U.S. processors or become American-made magnets. Shipment, processing and end-use data are needed to measure the benefit.",
"What Brazil retains: Brazil retains regulatory jurisdiction, the physical mine, local employment and potential tax and supplier benefits. Its government also seeks domestic value-added processing and relationships with multiple trade partners. A U.S.-linked purchase contract is not the same as American sovereignty over Brazilian mineral resources.",
"The unanswered test: The mine existed before the U.S. policy shift. The measurable question is whether the acquisition and government-backed agreements increase output, redirect shipments, build U.S. processing and magnet capacity, or reduce exposure to China compared with what would otherwise have occurred. Public commitments are not proof of those outcomes."],
